tel 1.0

base eucalyptus
import soundness

info   loggers, log formats and log destinations for the typed events that `Log` emits
detail
    _Eucalyptus_ is the runtime half of Soundness's typed logging. A method which logs states
    so in its type (`Long logs Backup.Event`) and emits a typed event with `Log.info`,
    `Log.warn` and the rest; those names, with `Level`, `Loggable` and the `LogSink` trait,
    live in `anticipation`, below every library which logs. Eucalyptus supplies what an
    *application* adds: a `Logger`, which is a `LogSink` placed in scope as a given, a log
    format (an `Inscribable`) which renders each event, and destinations to write it to.

    Every `Logger` in scope receives every event it admits, so routing is a matter of which
    loggers are in scope, each with its own threshold, categories, format and destination.
    With none in scope, logging is silent and free: the argument to every `Log` method is
    by name, and is never evaluated unless some logger accepts its level.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import logFormats.timestampedLogFormat
    import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
    import probates.awaitProbate
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory
    import threads.virtualThreads

    val logFile = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "app.log"

    supervise:
      given Logger[Any, Message] = Logger(logFile, level = Level.Info)
      Log.fine(m"not written: below the threshold")
      Log.info(m"written, with a timestamp and the level")
    ```

    A `Logger` writes on a background daemon, so it needs the `Monitor` which `supervise`
    provides, a `Probate` (from `probates`) for how that daemon is settled at the end of
    the supervision, and a `Writable` for its destination. Its format is chosen by importing
    one given from `logFormats` by name, and `logging.silentLogging` silences a whole file;
    both are choice packages, imported by name because `import logFormats.*` does not import
    givens.

    Writing to the terminal needs a destination which is writable by the format's form.
    `Out` is writable only by `Teletype`, through `writables.outTeletypeWritable` from
    escapade, so a terminal logger uses the `Teletype` format `ansiTimestampedLogFormat`
    with a `LogPalette`; there is no `Writable` for `Out` by `Text`, so `Logger(Out)` with
    `timestampedLogFormat` does not compile.

    The library has four source sets:

    - `eucalyptus.core` — `Logger`, `Inscribable`, `mute`, `Taggable`, the text formats
      `timestampedLogFormat` and `untimestampedLogFormat`, and `logging.silentLogging`.
    - `eucalyptus.ansi` — `LogPalette` and the terminal format `ansiTimestampedLogFormat`.
    - `eucalyptus.gcp` — `googleCloudPlatformLogFormat`, one JSON object per event, in the
      shape Google Cloud's logging agent reads.
    - `eucalyptus.syslog` — `Syslog`, a destination which hands lines to the system log
      through the `logger` command.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `logFormats` and `logging`
    choice packages merge with the umbrella's own.

refer eucalyptus.Logger
refer eucalyptus.Inscribable
refer eucalyptus.logFormats
refer eucalyptus.logging
refer anticipation.Log
refer anticipation.Loggable
refer anticipation.LogSink

entry .Logger   a log sink which renders each event through a format and writes it to a destination
  detail
      A `Logger[Event, Form]` is a `LogSink`: placed in scope as a given, it receives every
      event of type `Event` (or a subtype) logged in that scope, at or above its threshold
      level. The first type parameter selects which events it takes — `Any` for all — and
      the second is the carrier they are transcribed to before formatting, usually
      `Message`, which every `Communicable` event becomes.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
      import logFormats.untimestampedLogFormat
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory
      import threads.virtualThreads

      val detailed = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "detailed.log"
      val network = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "network.log"

      supervise:
        given everything: Logger[Any, Message] = Logger(detailed, level = Level.Fine)
        given networking: Logger[Any, Message] = Logger(network, categories = Set(Log.Network))
        Log.fine(m"only the detailed log sees this")
      ```

      Several loggers may be in scope at once, and `Loggable.fanOut` delivers each event to
      all of those which accept it. A logger accepts an event when its level is at or above
      the logger's threshold (`#accepts`) and, if the logger was given categories, when the
      event's runtime type mixes in one of them (`admits`); the event is constructed and
      formatted only if some logger accepts it.

      Formatting happens on the logging thread, and writing on a daemon: each event becomes
      one record on a queue, which the daemon streams to the destination. A failed write
      (`Truncation.Error`) ends that stream, and the daemon begins a fresh one from the same
      queue, so a transient failure does not silence the logger; the daemon stops when the
      VM shuts down.
  refer eucalyptus.logFormats
  refer anticipation.LogSink
  refer anticipation.Log
  entry .apply   makes a logger writing to a destination, with a threshold level and optional categories
      `Logger(destination, level, name, categories)`. The `level` defaults to `Level.Info`,
      and `categories` to the empty set, which admits events of every category; a non-empty
      set of `Log.Category`s (`Log.Network`, `Log.Filesystem`, …) admits only events whose
      runtime type mixes in one of their marker traits. `name` is accepted but not yet used.

      It needs in scope a format, `Form is Inscribable in Format` for the logger's carrier
      (one of the `logFormats` givens), a `Writable` for the destination by arrays of that
      format (any destination writable by `Text` qualifies, through `Writable.chunked`), a
      `Buffering`, a `Monitor` (from `supervise`), a `Probate` and a `Codepoint`, which the
      compiler supplies.

      The queue and the writer daemon belong to the **call site**: every `Logger` built at
      the same source position shares one, so a given re-evaluated on each summon does not
      start a new thread each time. The consequence is that the destination is fixed by the
      first call at that position: a helper which builds `Logger(path)` for different paths
      from one line writes all of them to the first path. Build loggers for different
      destinations at different call sites.
  entry #accepts   whether the logger records events at a level: at or above its threshold
  entry #submit   queues a transcribed event for formatting and writing, at a level and timestamp
      Called by `Loggable.fanOut` once `#accepts` and `admits` have passed; the event is
      formatted on the calling thread and its record queued for the writer daemon. Not
      usually called directly.

entry .Inscribable   a log format: how a value renders, with its level and timestamp, as one record
  detail
      A typeclass, `Message is Inscribable in Text`, whose `#formatter` turns a value, its
      `Level` and its timestamp (milliseconds since the epoch) into one record of the type
      `Form`. A `Logger` uses the one in scope for its carrier type; the standard ones are
      imported by name from `logFormats`. A custom format is a lambda:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given terse: Message is Inscribable in Text = (message, level, timestamp) =>
        t"${level.ordinal}: ${message.text}\n"
      ```

      A text format should end each record with a newline, since a destination writes the
      records one after another.
  refer eucalyptus.logFormats
  entry #formatter   renders a value with its level and timestamp as one record
  entry #contramap   a format for another type, which converts it to this one first
      `format.contramap[Event](_.message)` formats an `Event` by mapping it to the type this
      format handles; the level and timestamp pass through unchanged.
  entry #format   renders a value as a record, as an extension method on the value
      `message.format(level, timestamp)`, the same as `#formatter`.

entry .Taggable   a typeclass for values to which a tag of type `Operand` can be added
  detail
      `value.tag(operand)` returns the value with a tag added. Nothing in Soundness provides
      or uses an instance yet.
  entry #tag   the value with a tag added

entry .mute   runs a block with logging of one event type suppressed, whatever loggers are in scope
    `mute[Event]:` evaluates its block with a silent `Event is Loggable` given to it, which
    outranks the `Loggable.fanOut` the loggers in scope would otherwise provide, so events
    of exactly that type are dropped within the block while every other event is logged as
    usual. Events transcribed into another type before they are logged are not affected.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*

    object Backup:
      enum Event:
        case Started(source: Text)

      object Event:
        given communicable: Event is Communicable =
          case Started(source) => m"backing up $source"

    def backup(source: Text): Unit logs Backup.Event = Log.info(Backup.Event.Started(source))

    mute[Backup.Event]:
      backup(t"/var")
    ```

entry .dateFormat   the date and time pattern of the timestamped formats: `2026-Nov-04 09:30:00.250`
    A `java.text.SimpleDateFormat` for `yyyy-MMM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS` in the JVM's default time
    zone, used by `timestampedLogFormat` and `ansiTimestampedLogFormat`. A
    `SimpleDateFormat` is not safe for concurrent use, so it should not be called directly
    from several threads.

entry .LogPalette   the colours `ansiTimestampedLogFormat` paints levels and dates with
    A `Palette` in `Srgb` with four further colours: `error` for `Fail`, `warning` for
    `Warn`, `informative` for `Info`, and `subdued` for `Fine` and for the date. No palette
    is provided, so a terminal logger defines one as a given.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    given LogPalette = new Palette:
      type Form = Srgb
      def background: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.Black
      def foreground: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.White
      def error: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Red
      def warning: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.Orange
      def informative: Color in Srgb = WebColors.SteelBlue
      def subdued: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.DimGray
    ```

entry .Syslog   the system log as a log destination, with an optional tag on each line
  detail
      `Syslog(tag)` is a destination for a `Logger` with a `Text` format: its records are
      piped to the standard input of the `logger` command, with `-t tag` when a tag is
      given, so they arrive in the system log as `logger` writes them. A failure to launch
      `logger` or to write to it is ignored rather than raised, and the launch itself is
      not logged.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logFormats.untimestampedLogFormat
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import threads.virtualThreads

      supervise:
        given Logger[Any, Message] = Logger(Syslog(t"myapp"), level = Level.Warn)
        Log.warn(m"disk nearly full")
      ```

      A format without a timestamp suits it, as the system log adds its own. The `logger`
      command must be on the path: this is for Unix-like systems.
  entry .writable   `Syslog` is `Writable` by `Text`: each stream is piped to a `logger` process
      Needs a `Monitor` in scope, which the `Logger`'s `supervise` block provides. The text
      is encoded in the system's default codepage.
  entry #tag   the tag `logger -t` puts on each line, or `Unset` for none

entry .EucalyptusGcp   an empty marker object of the `eucalyptus.gcp` source set
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; the Google Cloud format is `logFormats.googleCloudPlatformLogFormat`.

entry .logFormats   the standard log formats, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Logger` renders each event through the `Inscribable` given in scope for its
      carrier, and none is in implicit scope: one is imported by name, since `import
      logFormats.*` does not import givens.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Timestamped text
        code
            import soundness.*
            import logFormats.timestampedLogFormat

            val format = summon[Message is Inscribable in Text]
            format.formatter(m"server listening on 8080", Level.Info, System.currentTimeMillis)
            // t"2026-Nov-04 09:30:00.250 [INFO] server listening on 8080\n"
      frame
        label Untimestamped text
        code
            import soundness.*
            import logFormats.untimestampedLogFormat

            val format = summon[Message is Inscribable in Text]
            format.formatter(m"server listening on 8080", Level.Info, System.currentTimeMillis)
            // t"[INFO] server listening on 8080\n"
      frame
        label Google Cloud JSON
        code
            import soundness.*
            import logFormats.googleCloudPlatformLogFormat

            val format = summon[Message is Inscribable in Text]
            format.formatter(m"server listening on 8080", Level.Info, System.currentTimeMillis)
            // t"""{"severity":"INFO","message":"server listening on 8080"}"""
      ##
      ```

      `timestampedLogFormat` and `untimestampedLogFormat` format any `Communicable` value,
      `ansiTimestampedLogFormat` and `googleCloudPlatformLogFormat` only a `Message`, which a
      `Logger[…, Message]` transcribes every event to. `textLevelLogFormat` and
      `teletypeableLogFormat`, which render a `Level`, are used by the others and are not
      exported to the umbrella's `logFormats`.
  entry .timestampedLogFormat   a line of text: date and time, the level in brackets, and the message
      `2026-Nov-04 09:30:00.250 [INFO] server listening on 8080`, ending with a newline, for
      any `Communicable` event, with the timestamp formatted by `dateFormat`.
  entry .untimestampedLogFormat   a line of text: the level in brackets, and the message
      `[INFO] server listening on 8080`, ending with a newline, for any `Communicable`
      event. Suits a destination which adds its own timestamp, such as `Syslog`.
  entry .textLevelLogFormat   renders a `Level` as `FINE`, `INFO`, `WARN` or `FAIL`
      The `Showable` for `Level` the text formats interpolate. It is not exported to the
      umbrella's `logFormats` package; import it as `eucalyptus.logFormats.textLevelLogFormat`.
  entry .ansiTimestampedLogFormat   a teletype: dimmed date and time, the level on a coloured ground, `>` and the message
      A `Message is Inscribable in Teletype`, for terminals, in `eucalyptus.ansi`. It needs a
      `LogPalette` in scope for its colours. Messages are cut into lines of at most 76
      characters, and each continuation line is indented to align under the first line's
      message. With `Out` as the destination it also needs `writables.outTeletypeWritable`
      and a `Stdio`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logFormats.ansiTimestampedLogFormat
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import termcapDefinitions.xterm256Termcap
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import writables.outTeletypeWritable

      given LogPalette = new Palette:
        type Form = Srgb
        def background: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.Black
        def foreground: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.White
        def error: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Red
        def warning: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.Orange
        def informative: Color in Srgb = WebColors.SteelBlue
        def subdued: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.DimGray

      supervise:
        given Logger[Any, Message] = Logger(Out)
        Log.warn(m"retrying after timeout")
      ```

      The records carry no trailing newline, so consecutive events written to `Out` are not
      separated by one.
  entry .teletypeableLogFormat   renders a `Level` as a bold label on the `LogPalette` colour for its level
      The `Teletypeable` for `Level` that `ansiTimestampedLogFormat` uses, in
      `eucalyptus.ansi`. It needs a `LogPalette`, and is not exported to the umbrella's
      `logFormats` package.
  entry .googleCloudPlatformLogFormat   one compact JSON object per event, with `severity` and `message`
      A `Message is Inscribable in Text`, in `eucalyptus.gcp`, which writes
      `{"severity":"WARNING","message":"…"}`, the structured form Google Cloud's logging
      agent reads from a container's standard output. The levels map to `DEBUG`, `INFO`,
      `WARNING` and `ERROR`; the message is its plain text, without styling. No timestamp is
      written, as the agent records its own, and the record has no trailing newline.

entry .logging   loggers to import by name, overriding those in scope
  detail
      A choice package, merged with the umbrella's `logging`. A given imported by name
      outranks the companion-scoped `Loggable.fanOut` which the loggers in scope provide.
  entry .silentLogging   discards every event, for the whole scope it is imported into
      `import logging.silentLogging` supplies a silent `Loggable` for every event type, so
      nothing logged in the file reaches any `Logger`, and no event is ever constructed.
      It suits a test or a script which calls a library that logs but whose output is
      unwanted; for one event type within one block, `mute` is finer.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging

      Log.warn(m"discarded")
      ```
